    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Right unit for each concern.** `DeleteOwner` keeps its single responsibility (the soft-delete step) and its name still fits the `Delete<E>` convention \u2014 a soft-delete is still conceptually \"delete this owner.\" The flag/getter/setter sit alongside the existing `possibleDuplicateOf` field in `Owner` in exactly the same style. Schema changes are mirrored consistently across all four DB dialects, and the OpenAPI `deleted` property is `readOnly` like its neighbors.\n\n- **The duplicated `if (existing.isDeleted()) continue;` guard is appropriate here, not a smell.** The two Check classes are separate OfficeFloor function steps that each already iterate `ownerRepository.findAll()` independently \u2014 that loop duplication is inherent to the one-function-per-step design, predating this change. Adding a matching two-line skip to each is the minimal change that stays with the grain; extracting a shared \"non-deleted owners\" helper across two step classes would be over-engineering for a guard this small and would cut against the per-step structure the codebase deliberately uses.\n\n- **Documentation is well-placed.** The Javadoc on `DeleteOwner` explains the soft-delete contract and cross-links the two checks that depend on it; the comments on the guards state the \"why,\" matching the existing commentary style in both files.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find all of this natural \u2014 no logic in the wrong place, no class becoming a catch-all, nothing duplicated beyond what the step-per-function design already implies.",
